{"type": "message", "id": "531c443c", "parentId": "00087324", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T09:06:17.378Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_RC1Qpt1TjQSKjIZ38ztcSfVa", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"5-year survival rate lung cancer 5-year relative survival United States SEER 2024 5-year survival lung cancer percentage site:cancer.org 'lung cancer survival rate 5-year'\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 5,\n  \"tookMs\": 844,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ef092808fbfa7ef3\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nLung Cancer Survival Rates | 5-Year Survival Rates for Lung Cancer | American Cancer Society\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ef092808fbfa7ef3\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/lung-cancer/detection-diagnosis-staging/survival-rates.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7047d8b224b4d731\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor example, if the 5-year relative survival rate for a specific stage of lung cancer is 60%, it means that <strong>people who have that cancer are, on average, about 60%</strong> as likely as people who don’t have that cancer to live for at least 5 years after being diagnosed.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7047d8b224b4d731\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c209df42916cb9e0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nAL 30,030 AZ 42,560 AR 19,700 CA 199,980 CO 29,020 CT 23,920 DE 7,680 FL\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c209df42916cb9e0\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.org/content/dam/cancer-org/research/cancer-facts-and-statistics/annual-cancer-facts-and-figures/2025/2025-cancer-facts-and-figures-acs.pdf\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"55007b6bf064cf84\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nData source: DevCan: Probability of Developing or Dying of Cancer Software, Version 6.9.0. Statistical Research and Applications Branch, National Cancer Institute, 2024. ... However, like all screening tests, it is not perfect. ... Table 7. Trends in 5-year Relative Survival Rates (%) by Race, US, 1975-2020 ... Rates are age adjusted for normal life expectancy and based on cases diagnosed in the SEER 9 areas for 1975-1977 and 1995-1997 and SEER 22 areas, excluding Illinois\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"55007b6bf064cf84\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"642eb26f71402511\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nLung Cancer Statistics | How Common Is Lung Cancer? | American Cancer Society\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"642eb26f71402511\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/lung-cancer/about/key-statistics.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"38b6b9352e6d9903\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nGet the American Cancer Society&#x27;s latest statistics on lung cancer. Learn how common lung cancer is and what your lifetime chance of getting it may be.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"38b6b9352e6d9903\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 13, 2026\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"75bb9faf2c810880\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nACS Annual Statistics Report: Milestone 70 Percent 5-Year Survival Rate for all Cancers Combined; Largest Gains for Advanced and Fatal Cancers\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"75bb9faf2c810880\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pressroom.cancer.org/cancer-statistics-report-2026\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fa4e4a5cc3b7fcc1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSurvival gains since the mid-1990s are especially notable for people diagnosed with more fatal cancers, such as myeloma (from 32% to 62%), liver cancer (7% to 22%), and lung cancer (<strong>15% to 28%).</strong>\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fa4e4a5cc3b7fcc1\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 13, 2026\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pressroom.cancer.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"40cc98b06b547c60\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSurvival Rates for Lung Carcinoid Tumors | American Cancer Society\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"40cc98b06b547c60\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/lung-carcinoid-tumor/detection-diagnosis-staging/survival-rates.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2bd0872c9cd1413d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor example, if the 5-year relative survival rate for a specific stage of lung carcinoid tumor is 90%, it means that <strong>people who have that cancer are, on average, about 90%</strong> as likely as people who don’t have that cancer to live for at least 5 years after being diagnosed.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2bd0872c9cd1413d\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.org\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}], "details": {"query": "5-year survival rate lung cancer 5-year relative survival United States SEER 2024 5-year survival lung cancer percentage site:cancer.org 'lung cancer survival rate 5-year'", "provider": "brave", "count": 5, "tookMs": 844, "externalContent": {"untrusted": true, "source": "web_search", "provider": "brave", "wrapped": true}, "results": [{"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ef092808fbfa7ef3\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nLung Cancer Survival Rates | 5-Year Survival Rates for Lung Cancer | American Cancer Society\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ef092808fbfa7ef3\">>>", "url": "https://