{"type": "message", "id": "fb8ff2f4", "parentId": "7dfe24c2", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T09:08:21.093Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_L38ocKvj7RKY4uzhtFy7kk3e", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"chordoma cases per year United States annual cases 'chordoma' incidence per year US 'cases per year'\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 956,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7404426abedb54e4\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDescriptive epidemiology of chordomas in the United States - PubMed\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7404426abedb54e4\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32342333/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"070e4a7805f03fb1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe most common location was cranial (38.7%), followed by sacral (34.3%) and spinal (27.0%). The average age-adjusted incidence rate was <strong>0.088 per 100,000 persons per year</strong> (95% CI 0.086-0.091), with an annual percentage change of 1.29% (95% ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"070e4a7805f03fb1\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"23632085383a52f1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nChordoma: incidence and survival patterns in the United States, 1973-1995 - PubMed\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"23632085383a52f1\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11227920/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8280a6386f27e7e5\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nResults: The age-adjusted chordoma incidence rate (IR) of <strong>0.08 per 100,000</strong> was age-dependent, more common in males (IR 0.10) than females (IR 0.06) and rare among patients aged &lt;40 years and blacks.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8280a6386f27e7e5\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1234f61bbeb72dc7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nChordoma: Current status, problems, and future directions - ScienceDirect\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1234f61bbeb72dc7\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S014702722100088X\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5d59ad0690ce73fa\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nChordoma is an extremely rare cancer with an annual incidence of approximately <strong>1 per 1,000,000 per year</strong> in the USA and Europe, leading to about 350 cases annually.1,2 The presumed cellular origin of chordoma is the residual embryonic notochord.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5d59ad0690ce73fa\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"July 1, 2021\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.sciencedirect.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"891de99892530fae\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nPopulation-based survival analysis of primary spinal chordoma in the US from 2000 to 2020 - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"891de99892530fae\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11538232/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8ad4aecdb8e665af\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nChordomas are rare, slow-growing, ... nerves, and vascular structures. The annual incidence rate of new chordoma diagnoses is approximately <strong>one to two cases per million</strong> each year, with a slightly higher frequency in males than females [2]....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8ad4aecdb8e665af\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n