{"type": "message", "id": "c9cd26b4", "parentId": "d73dc8b0", "timestamp": "2026-04-04T09:08:58.988Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_cXizrDn1l8HkJiTBw5nggg9O", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"thymoma cases per year United States annual cases thymoma incidence 'cases per year' 'US'\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 991,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"dafbe256884a767c\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nKey Statistics About Thymus Cancers | American Cancer Society\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"dafbe256884a767c\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/thymus-cancer/about/key-statistics.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3a1c7f1d8de814d0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nAlthough thymic tumors (thymomas and thymic carcinomas) are the most common tumors in the anterior mediastinum (the front part of the space between the lungs), overall they are rare. They occur at a rate of only <strong>1.3 cases for every million people</strong> each year in the US.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3a1c7f1d8de814d0\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"December 6, 2024\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9eef95b3b4571d25\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nTrends in the incidence of thymoma, thymic carcinoma, and thymic neuroendocrine tumor in the United States - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9eef95b3b4571d25\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6938371/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a020b05a6f25bfc0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nAccording to the USCS database, the age standardized rate of thymic cancer increased from 0.23/100,000 in 2001 to 0.30/100,000 in 2015, with AAPC of 1.8% (p &lt; 0.05), and that of thymoma increased from 0.16/100,000 in 2001 to <strong>0.19/100,000 in 2015,</strong> with an AAPC of 1.0% (p &lt; 0.05).\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a020b05a6f25bfc0\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9774d788d4207ab5\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFrontiers | Epidemiology of thymomas and thymic carcinomas in the United States and Germany, 1999-2019\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9774d788d4207ab5\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/oncology/articles/10.3389/fonc.2023.1308989/full\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5589290f15d72199\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nAmong patients aged ≥20 years, ... and TETs (10%/27%). The overall annual incidence of thymoma was <strong>2.2/2.64 (US/GER) per million inhabitants</strong> and for thymic carcinomas 0.48/0.42....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5589290f15d72199\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"December 19, 2023\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.frontiersin.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4ebfc3c82004db41\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEpidemiology of thymoma and associated malignancies - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"4ebfc3c82004db41\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2951303/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2a34eccf02bd110d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nBased on SEER data, the overall ... U.S. is <strong>0.13 per 100,000 person-years (Table 1). Although this estimate may be too low for the reasons enumerated above, it is nonetheless apparent that thymoma is a rare cancer. For example, a simple extrapolation to the U.S. population (300 million people) would lead to an estimate of 390 cases per year</strong> in the entire ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2a34eccf02bd110d\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c02bbfa98fb4e3d8\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEpidemiology of thymoma - PMC - NIH\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c02bbfa98fb4e3d8\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7797839/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c2dbc2e80e5e7bdc\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n<strong>incidence peaks in the 7th decade</strong> (7). Engels et al. used registry data from the American National Cancer Institute’s Surveillance, Epidemiology and End Results (SEER) programme, between 1973–1998. This database reflects 26% of the American population, and is based on reported malignant ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c2dbc2e80e5e7bdc\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0f07dfec944a41d4\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nMalignant thymoma in the United States: Demographic patterns in incidence and associations with subsequent malignancies - Engels - 2003 - International Journal of Cancer - Wiley Online Library\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0f07dfec944a41d4\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/ijc.11099\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1c61c9d5dea0972a\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe overall incidence of malignant thymoma was 0.15 per 100,000 person-years (<strong>849 cases</strong>). Thymoma incidence increased into the 8th decade of age and then decreased. Incidence was higher in males than females (p=0.007) and was highest among Asians/Pacific Islanders (0.49 per 100,000 person-years).\\n<<<END